<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#poliudie">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Poliudie</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as poliudia, dar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The poliudie was the practice by which the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> collected tribute from their East Slavic subjects. In the mid-10th century the Byzantine emperor Constantine Porphyrogenitus described how, from November to April, the Rus' chiefs left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> on these 'rounds' among the tributary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, who paid in money, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>, other goods or slaves. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> relates that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olga-of-kiev">Olga of Kiev</a> changed the method of collection after the Drevlians killed her husband Igor in 945 for demanding more than had been agreed. Collection later moved to stations called pogosty, and from the 12th to the 16th centuries the term denoted a fixed tax, usually paid in furs or money.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/poliudie.jpg" alt="Poliudie" width="330" height="235" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nicholas Roerich · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Russian_prince_takes_tribute_by_Roerich.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
